EXT. BRANNOCK BAY SALT PANS - DAWN

NARRATOR (V.O.)
On the edge of Brannock Bay, the day starts before the sun does.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
For three hundred years, families here have farmed the sea, one shallow pan at a time.

EDDA
My grandmother used to say salt is just seawater that learned patience.
You can't hurry it. You can only stay out of its way.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
In the nineteen fifties, more than two hundred people worked these flats.
Today, there are eleven.

JONAH
People ask why I'd want a job that's this slow.
Honestly? It's the only place my phone doesn't work.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Each evening, the harvest is raked into white hills, and the bay begins again.
